        "description": "Rank 1, theoretically rather than empirically: Relational descriptions can independently make an innovation legitimate for organizational consideration and reveal useful applications involving people, tasks, and environments. Legitimacy and usefulness each increase expressed adoption interest; neither must precede the other. Shared evaluator dispositions can correlate all three ratings without explaining away these possible causal pathways. Framing benefits should be greatest where organizational authorization and credible applications are both open to reinterpretation. Benefits may weaken or reverse where disability-specific descriptions establish purchasing obligations or communicate essential specialized capabilities. These contextual predictions are hypotheses, not findings.",
